t slopes, each also passing through a point in column 2,3,4 maybe. But each line can pass through at most one point per column, but they can share the same point (5,1). So we could have line S1: through (5,1) and (2,1) (horizontal? Actually (5,1) and (2,1) are horizontal, slope 0, not sunny). So not allowed. S1: through (5,1) and (2,2): slope = (2-1)/(2-5) = 1/(-3) = -1/3, sunny. That line also passes through (3,? ) maybe not integer. So S1 covers (5,1) and (2,2). S2: through (5,1) and (2,3): slope = (3-1)/(2-5) = 2/(-3) = -2/3, sunny. S3: through (5,1) and (2,4): slope = (4-1)/(2-5) = 3/(-3) = -1, not sunny. So can't. So we can have at most two sunny lines through (5,1) that also intersect column 2 at points (2,2) and (2,3). We could also have lines through (5,1) and column 3 points: (3,1) slope = (1-1)/(3-5) = 0/(-2) = 0, not sunny. (3,2) slope = (2-1)/(3-5) = 1/(-2) = -0.5, sunny. (3,3) slope = (3-1)/(3-5) = 2/(-2) = -1, not sunny. So we can have a sunny line through (5,1) and (3,2). That's a third sunny line. So we have three sunny lines through (5,1): S1: (5,1)-(2,2), S2: (5,1)-(2,3), S3: (5,1)-(3,2). That's three distinct sunny lines. We need a fourth sunny line. It could also pass through (5,1) and some other point, but we need to avoid slopes 0,∞,-1. The remaining points in column 2 are (2,1) (horizontal), (2,4) (slope -1). In column 3 we have (3,1) (horizontal), (3,3) (slope -1). So we cannot use those. So we cannot have a fourth sunny line through (5,1) that also passes through a point in column 2 or 3 without hitting slope 0 or -1. So we need to consider a line that passes through (5,1) and a point in column 4: (4,1) slope 0 (horizontal), not allowed; (4,2) slope = (2-1)/(4-5) = 1/(-1) = -1, not allowed. So no sunny line through (5,1) and column 4. So we cannot have a fourth sunny line that passes through (5,1) and any other point in T_n without violating sunny condition. So we cannot have 4 distinct sunny lines all passing through (5,1). So we need to have at least one sunny line that does not pass through (5,1). But then (5,1) would be uncovered unless we have a non-sunny line covering it. We have one non-sunny line (vertical line at column 1). That vertical line does not cover (5,1). So we need to cover (5,1) with a sunny line. So we need at least one sunny line that passes through (5,1). But we can have at most 3 sunny lines that pass through (5,1) (as we found). So we need a fourth sunny line that does not pass through (5,1). But then (5,1) would be covered only by the three sunny lines we have, which is okay because it's covered at least once. So we can have 4 sunny lines: three of them pass through (5,1) and cover some points in columns 2 and 3; the fourth sunny line does not pass through (5,1) but covers other points. Let's try to construct such a configuration.\n\nWe have vertical line x=1 covering column 1 points (1,1)-(1,5). Then we need to cover the remaining points:\n\nColumn 2: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4)\nColumn 3: (3,1),(3,2),(3,3)\nColumn 4: (4,1),(4,2)\nColumn 5: (5,1)\n\nWe have 4 sunny lines: S1, S2, S3, S4.\n\nWe can let S1: line through (5,1) and (2,2) slope -1/3 (sunny). This line also passes through (3,? ) maybe not integer. So S1 covers (5,1) and (2,2).\n\nS2: line through (5,1) and (2,3) slope -2/3 (sunny). Covers (5,1) and (2,3).\n\nS3: line through (5,1) and (3,2) slope -1/2 (sunny). Covers (5,1) and (3,2).\n\nNow we need to cover the remaining points: (2,1),(2,4),(3,1),(3,3),(4,1),(4,2). Also (2,2),(2,3),(3,2) are already covered by S1,S2,S3. So we have 6 points left. We have one sunny line left (S4) and possibly we could also use the vertical line x=1 (non-sunny) to cover some of these points? No, vertical line x=1 only covers column 1 points, which are already covered. So we need S4 to cover the remaining 6 points. But a single line cannot cover 6 points across multiple columns unless they are collinear. Let's see if we can find a line that passes through many of these points. For example,