lines. If we have many sunny lines, we need to use vertical lines for the remaining columns. The columns are n in number. If we have k sunny lines, we need at most n-k vertical lines (or other non-sunny lines). But we have n lines total, so we have n-k non-sunny lines. The vertical lines can be placed at some columns. But we need to cover all points. Let's think about column counting: Each column must be covered by at least one line. If we have a sunny line, it can cover at most one point in each column, but not necessarily cover the column fully. So each column must have at least one line that covers all points in that column (i.e., a vertical line). But maybe a column can be covered by a combination of a sunny line covering one point and a horizontal line covering the rest? But horizontal lines are not vertical, but they are not sunny (they are parallel to x-axis). Horizontal lines can cover points across columns at a given y. So a column could be covered by a horizontal line covering some points in that column, plus maybe a sunny line covering another point, etc. So we need to consider covering each column's points using a mix of vertical, horizontal, slope -1, and sunny lines.\n\nBut the solution's claim \"Impossible by column counting\" suggests that if k≥4, we cannot cover all points because each column has at most one point covered by a sunny line, and the remaining points need to be covered by non-sunny lines, but there are not enough non-sunny lines to cover all columns. Let's examine.\n\nWe have n columns. Suppose we have k sunny lines. Each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, so across all sunny lines, we can cover at most k points in each column (maybe less). So each column still has at least (height - k) points left uncovered. Those must be covered by non-sunny lines. Non-sunny lines can be vertical, horizontal, or slope -1. Let's consider vertical lines: each vertical line can cover all remaining points in its column. So we could assign a vertical line to each column that still has uncovered points. But we have only n-k non-sunny lines. So we can assign at most n-k vertical lines. So we can cover at most n-k columns fully with vertical lines. The remaining columns must be covered by horizontal or slope -1 lines. But horizontal lines can cover points across many columns at a given y. However, each horizontal line can cover at most one point per column (since it's horizontal, it intersects each column at exactly one point). Actually a horizontal line y = b intersects each column x = a at point (a,b). So it can cover at most one point per column, but can cover many columns. Similarly, a slope -1 line (x+y = c) intersects each column at at most one point (since solving x = a, then y = c - a). So also at most one per column. So any non-vertical line (including horizontal and slope -1) can cover at most one point per column. So vertical lines are the only ones that can cover multiple points in a column. So to cover all points in a column, we need a vertical line for that column (or a combination of multiple non-vertical lines covering each point individually). But we have limited number of lines.\n\nThus, to cover all points, we need at least as many vertical lines as there are columns that have more than k points (since each column can have at most k points covered by sunny lines). Actually each column has height h = n+1-a. If h > k, then after using k sunny lines (each covering at most one point per column), there remain at least h - k points in that column. Those must be covered by non-sunny lines. Since each non-vertical line can cover at most one point per column, we would need at least h - k non-vertical lines dedicated to that column (or vertical lines). But we have only n - k non-sunny lines total. So we need to check if we can allocate them across columns.\n\nIf k≥4, then for columns with height > k, we need many non-sunny lines. Let's compute heights: column a has height n+1-a. For a=1, height 